1
00:00:02,147 --> 00:00:07,220
What vertex information represents3D shape and
lighting directions?

2
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:13,120
Inspect game roofs and rocks, then calculate
indexed connections and memory size.

3
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:16,040
Compare face and vertex normals and follow the

4
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:20,200
complete neighboring-face accumulation
algorithm.

5
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:23,620
Finally handle sharp edges and triangulation
bias

6
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,460
and verify the answers on an explicitly
defined mesh.

7
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:30,540
Traverse a roof in Sunset Overdrive.

8
00:00:32,002 --> 00:00:36,082
Separate its sloped outline from repeated
lines across the surface.

9
00:00:37,763 --> 00:00:40,803
Moving sideways reveals roof and wall faces.

10
00:00:41,063 --> 00:00:46,465
It is easy to confuse the surface shape with
its pattern.

11
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:51,271
The next roof has panels and vents.

12
00:00:51,631 --> 00:00:54,991
Distinguish shape that changes the outline
from lines inside a face.

13
00:00:55,736 --> 00:01:00,036
Rendered pixels cannot prove that each line is
a geometric

14
00:01:00,036 --> 00:01:03,761
edge: geometry and textures can both create
lines.

15
00:01:04,645 --> 00:01:08,505
We therefore define our own small mesh to
explain storage,

16
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:12,150
without guessing this game hidden vertex
count.

17
00:01:12,850 --> 00:01:15,770
Shapes have multiple representations.

18
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:21,750
CSG combines solids using union, intersection
and difference.

19
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:25,550
Subtracting a cylinder from a box makes an
opening in our solid diagram;

20
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:30,350
it does not identify a game implementation.

21
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:34,210
Metaballs and implicit functions offer other
volume descriptions;

22
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:38,830
representation and display-surface storage
serve different purposes.

23
00:01:39,170 --> 00:01:42,830
Triangle meshes connect planar surface pieces;

24
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:47,670
they may represent open cloth or leaves rather
than closed solids.

25
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:50,870
Editing polygons can have varying vertex
counts.

26
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:56,070
Rendering triangulates them with the valid
methods developed earlier.

27
00:01:56,370 --> 00:02:00,870
We now focus on shared vertices rather than
one triangle,

28
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:04,050
translating vertex, edge and face
relationships into data.

29
00:02:04,733 --> 00:02:09,153
Split a quad into triangles012 and023

30
00:02:09,153 --> 00:02:14,173
using four vertices numbered0 through3.

31
00:02:14,425 --> 00:02:18,833
A triangle array stores three records per
face,

32
00:02:19,153 --> 00:02:23,653
duplicating vertices0 and2 with their
attributes.

33
00:02:24,013 --> 00:02:27,513
Indexed storage separates vertex records from
integers,

34
00:02:27,753 --> 00:02:32,273
with three indices referencing the data for
each triangle.

35
00:02:32,313 --> 00:02:36,793
Assume32-byte vertices and2-byte indices.

36
00:02:37,093 --> 00:02:40,753
The triangle array uses6 times32, or192 bytes.

37
00:02:41,473 --> 00:02:45,533
Indexed storage uses4 times32 plus6

38
00:02:45,773 --> 00:02:49,033
times2:128 plus12 equals140 bytes.

39
00:02:49,293 --> 00:02:56,345
The source separate local example shares one
vertex among six

40
00:02:56,345 --> 00:03:03,253
faces:32 plus12 equals44 bytes, unlike the
whole quad140.

41
00:03:03,713 --> 00:03:09,853
Total savings depend on attributes, sharing
and index format;

42
00:03:09,853 --> 00:03:13,953
records may include normals, UVs, colors and
skinning data.

43
00:03:14,783 --> 00:03:18,323
An identical index shares a record;

44
00:03:18,503 --> 00:03:22,963
equal positions with different normals or UVs
require separate records.

45
00:03:23,283 --> 00:03:26,123
Declare the winding convention. The source
clockwise choice

46
00:03:26,443 --> 00:03:31,983
must agree with the renderer front-face
configuration.

47
00:03:32,263 --> 00:03:38,503
Cyclic012 to120 preserves orientation;
swapping two indices to021 reverses it.

48
00:03:39,043 --> 00:03:41,823
The triangles share edge0–2, but a plain index
list does

49
00:03:41,823 --> 00:03:46,103
not explicitly store neighboring face
references.

50
00:03:46,443 --> 00:03:51,283
Editors can store explicit edges and
adjacent-face references,

51
00:03:51,443 --> 00:03:53,303
as in winged-edge structures.

52
00:03:53,603 --> 00:03:58,343
Boundary edges may have one face and
nonmanifold edges more than two,

53
00:03:58,643 --> 00:04:01,763
so exactly-two adjacency is not universal.

54
00:04:02,083 --> 00:04:06,063
Triangles can be batched by compatible
attributes or materials;

55
00:04:06,323 --> 00:04:10,703
editor structures need not match GPU delivery
structures.

56
00:04:11,467 --> 00:04:16,407
Explore the green room in Big Walk,

57
00:04:16,407 --> 00:04:19,327
separating the black grid on a nearby panel
from curved walls and rocks.

58
00:04:21,953 --> 00:04:25,833
Moving views show panel boundaries and
internal lines,

59
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:32,560
separating visible shape from surface detail.

60
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:36,320
Circular openings and cylindrical bases appear
behind them,

61
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,820
placing straight patterns and curved outlines
together.

62
00:04:42,447 --> 00:04:45,967
Black lines do not prove triangle-index edges;

63
00:04:45,967 --> 00:04:51,573
they could be patterns, separate surfaces or
another representation.

64
00:04:51,853 --> 00:04:56,413
Our explicit index list proves the0–2
connection;

65
00:04:56,733 --> 00:05:02,160
these rendered game pixels do not expose such
a list.

66
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:06,380
Separate shape data from shading directions.

67
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:10,340
The next diagram keeps geometry fixed while
changing normals.

68
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:16,387
Observe the panel faces and outline during the
camera movement,

69
00:05:16,607 --> 00:05:20,687
motivating face and vertex normals.

70
00:05:21,467 --> 00:05:24,887
Move among rocks and cacti in Megabonk.

71
00:05:25,147 --> 00:05:29,167
Separate angular rock outlines from brightness
inside their faces.

72
00:05:29,447 --> 00:05:39,986
The moving view retains planar rock faces and
angular edges;

73
00:05:40,346 --> 00:05:44,526
an angular appearance does not expose their
normal treatment.

74
00:05:50,505 --> 00:05:55,405
Pass a large rock, then cacti and lower rocks;

75
00:05:55,625 --> 00:05:59,045
differently oriented surfaces can receive the
same directional light differently.

76
00:06:05,025 --> 00:06:07,885
Materials and shadows also affect brightness.
Our next diagram changes only

77
00:06:07,885 --> 00:06:13,745
normal direction rather than inferring its
numerical values from pixels.

78
00:06:19,664 --> 00:06:22,844
The final interval shows pillars and angular
roof forms.

79
00:06:23,064 --> 00:06:27,464
Treat geometry positions and lighting
directions as separate inputs.

80
00:06:33,263 --> 00:06:36,723
Can shading look smoother without moving
vertices?

81
00:06:36,883 --> 00:06:41,263
Compare face and vertex normals on our
original hexagonal prism.

82
00:06:41,883 --> 00:06:46,923
A face normal is perpendicular to its plane:
cross two edges,

83
00:06:46,923 --> 00:06:50,963
choose the declared orientation and normalize.

84
00:06:51,203 --> 00:06:58,183
Several planes meet at a vertex, so a sharp
corner has no unique geometric surface normal.

85
00:06:58,483 --> 00:07:02,823
For a smooth surface approximated by
triangles,

86
00:07:03,123 --> 00:07:04,963
vertex normals approximate its directions for
shading.

87
00:07:05,143 --> 00:07:09,103
A hexagonal prism can have smoother shading
with radial vertex

88
00:07:09,103 --> 00:07:13,163
normals while its positions and connections
remain unchanged.

89
00:07:13,163 --> 00:07:17,963
Dotting unit normal and unit light direction
gives a cosine;

90
00:07:18,303 --> 00:07:21,923
renormalization prevents vector length
contaminating that angle.

91
00:07:22,203 --> 00:07:25,143
Front-face configuration differs from shading
normals;

92
00:07:25,303 --> 00:07:31,543
distinguish winding/culling issues from
lighting-direction issues.

93
00:07:32,167 --> 00:07:35,387
Flat shading evaluates a face direction,

94
00:07:35,747 --> 00:07:39,007
allowing brightness discontinuities across
neighboring faces.

95
00:07:39,227 --> 00:07:44,087
Gouraud shading evaluates lighting at vertices
and

96
00:07:44,287 --> 00:07:46,547
interpolates the resulting values rather than
normals.

97
00:07:46,787 --> 00:07:49,987
Per-pixel shading interpolates vertex normals,

98
00:07:49,987 --> 00:07:52,827
renormalizes them and evaluates lighting at
covered samples.

99
00:07:53,027 --> 00:07:57,827
A half blend of right and up gives(.5,.5),

100
00:07:58,127 --> 00:08:01,187
whose length is about.707 rather than one.

101
00:08:01,187 --> 00:08:04,227
Normalizing produces components about.707;

102
00:08:04,587 --> 00:08:07,367
do this before using the interpolated
direction in lighting.

103
00:08:07,887 --> 00:08:11,447
The source Phong shading name refers to normal
interpolation,

104
00:08:11,947 --> 00:08:14,647
distinct from the Phong specular reflection
model.

105
00:08:15,027 --> 00:08:18,367
Smooth brightness does not change a hexagonal
silhouette;

106
00:08:18,687 --> 00:08:22,547
normals alone do not move the surface or
reshape holes.

107
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:26,660
Characters move on Big Walk red deck; observe
the broad floor,

108
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:30,980
curved rail and tilted control panels.

109
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:36,380
Smooth shading directions and geometric
boundaries differ;

110
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:40,120
distinguish intended smooth surfaces from hard
joins.

111
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:46,460
Now accumulate all neighboring face directions
and split sharing at the required boundaries.

112
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:50,820
Walk between yellow structures in Big Walk,

113
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:55,213
observing broad flat walls and circular
opening boundaries.

114
00:08:55,473 --> 00:08:58,613
Changing views reveal brightness and shadows;

115
00:08:59,133 --> 00:09:03,767
variation within a face differs from the
opening outline.

116
00:09:04,087 --> 00:09:07,867
A character passing through reveals thickness
and passage space,

117
00:09:08,187 --> 00:09:12,727
distinct from smooth shading directions.

118
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:17,420
We cannot infer vertex counts or normal
generation here;

119
00:09:17,420 --> 00:09:22,160
the question is why fixed coarse geometry can
shade more smoothly.

120
00:09:22,873 --> 00:09:26,813
Normals provide the answer. We next show the
complete

121
00:09:26,813 --> 00:09:30,693
accumulation of neighboring face directions.

122
00:09:31,207 --> 00:09:36,727
Flat walls and smooth curves need different
directions;

123
00:09:37,147 --> 00:09:40,387
unconditional averaging can incorrectly shade
sharp corners.

124
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:42,680
Revisit wall and opening boundaries and
consider which

125
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:46,840
directions should blend and which should
remain separate.

126
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:51,140
Generate simple averaged normals for a mesh
without them,

127
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:55,000
starting every vertex accumulator at zero.

128
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:57,780
Cross each triangle edges; reject or report

129
00:09:58,040 --> 00:10:03,020
nearly-zero degenerate faces before
normalization.

130
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:10,080
Normalize a valid face normal and add it to
each of the three indexed vertex accumulators.

131
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:16,000
One right, up and front direction sum
to(1,1,1);

132
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,580
dividing by square root three gives components
about.577.

133
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:23,640
After processing every face, normalize each
nonzero sum;

134
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:27,740
dividing by face count first preserves the
final direction.

135
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:31,380
Isolated vertices and canceling directions can
produce zero sums;

136
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:36,840
use explicit split, authored-normal or error
policies.

137
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:39,500
This basic algorithm gives each face one vote;

138
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:44,140
sharp boundaries and triangulation bias need
separate treatment.

139
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:47,590
Three cube faces meet at a corner;

140
00:10:47,810 --> 00:10:53,450
sharing one shading normal blends their
directions and brightness boundaries.

141
00:10:53,790 --> 00:10:57,730
The cube geometry stays fixed but can shade
like a rounded object;

142
00:10:58,010 --> 00:11:02,210
intentional hard edges must not blend
directions.

143
00:11:02,210 --> 00:11:04,990
Duplicate records at equal positions: a cube
with eight

144
00:11:05,011 --> 00:11:10,430
locations can use six faces times four, or24
records.

145
00:11:10,750 --> 00:11:14,390
Store the top normal in top-face records and
side normals in side records,

146
00:11:14,390 --> 00:11:21,030
updating indices accordingly.

147
00:11:21,111 --> 00:11:26,370
UV seams can require equal positions pointing
to different image locations,

148
00:11:26,790 --> 00:11:29,370
also requiring separate records.

149
00:11:29,790 --> 00:11:34,010
Opposing normals on two-sided leaves cancel to
zero;

150
00:11:34,370 --> 00:11:38,290
split the sides or define two-sided
orientation treatment explicitly.

151
00:11:38,770 --> 00:11:45,010
Duplicating records need not open a physical
gap;

152
00:11:45,010 --> 00:11:48,090
it separates attributes and logical references
while tools can preserve geometric adjacency.

153
00:11:48,716 --> 00:11:53,696
Suppose two top triangles and two side faces
meet at a vertex;

154
00:11:53,976 --> 00:11:57,736
equal face votes give the top direction two
votes.

155
00:11:57,896 --> 00:12:03,056
Axis-aligned votes sum to(1,2,1),

156
00:12:03,116 --> 00:12:06,516
normalized to approximately(.408,.816,.408),
biased upward.

157
00:12:06,816 --> 00:12:12,136
Changing a quad diagonal changes which
vertices touch two triangles,

158
00:12:12,376 --> 00:12:14,996
potentially altering averages without changing
the surface.

159
00:12:15,256 --> 00:12:18,296
Angle weighting uses the corner angle:
splitting a

160
00:12:18,296 --> 00:12:22,136
quad corner ninety degrees preserves its total
ninety.

161
00:12:22,516 --> 00:12:25,796
Accumulate angle times direction and
normalize;

162
00:12:26,176 --> 00:12:30,596
other policies such as area weighting
approximate different intended surfaces.

163
00:12:30,896 --> 00:12:34,816
Weighting does not fix intentionally sharp
cubes;

164
00:12:35,096 --> 00:12:39,296
first separate hard edges, then average only
intended smooth regions.

165
00:12:40,050 --> 00:12:42,470
Move to another opening in the yellow
structure,

166
00:12:42,730 --> 00:12:49,576
observing broad planes and curved boundaries
up close.

167
00:12:49,896 --> 00:12:54,456
Viewing a character through the opening
produces a clear

168
00:12:54,616 --> 00:12:59,036
silhouette boundary that smooth shading cannot
remove.

169
00:12:59,376 --> 00:13:05,163
Distinguish gradual surface directions from
sharp

170
00:13:05,363 --> 00:13:09,710
joins without measuring this model
vertex-record count.

171
00:13:09,710 --> 00:13:14,890
Our cube example split directions at equal
positions,

172
00:13:15,090 --> 00:13:18,870
whereas intended smooth interiors blend
neighboring directions.

173
00:13:20,276 --> 00:13:23,336
The choice aligns shading with shape,

174
00:13:23,776 --> 00:13:27,696
rather than universally minimizing records or
averaging every join.

175
00:13:29,283 --> 00:13:31,943
Now imagine stretching a shape horizontally:
normal directions must change,

176
00:13:32,243 --> 00:13:37,043
but applying the position transform directly
can fail.

177
00:13:38,530 --> 00:13:42,310
Next we verify perpendicularity with
explicitly defined tangent and normal values,

178
00:13:42,310 --> 00:13:47,430
without reverse-engineering brightness from
pixels.

179
00:13:48,050 --> 00:13:55,210
Four32-byte vertices and six2-byte indices
total140 bytes;

180
00:13:55,350 --> 00:13:58,630
distinguish whole-mesh storage from a local
shared-record calculation.

181
00:13:58,830 --> 00:14:01,790
One index shares an entire record;

182
00:14:01,950 --> 00:14:05,550
equal positions with different normals or UVs
require distinct records.

183
00:14:05,790 --> 00:14:10,910
The basic algorithm initializes, computes
valid face normals,

184
00:14:10,910 --> 00:14:15,190
accumulates at three vertices and normalizes
last, with explicit zero/degenerate handling.

185
00:14:15,190 --> 00:14:20,450
Split cube hard edges and choose a weighting
policy on smooth regions;

186
00:14:20,750 --> 00:14:23,830
averaging directions does not round
silhouettes.

187
00:14:24,070 --> 00:14:28,230
Next we transform normals while preserving
perpendicularity

188
00:14:28,230 --> 00:14:31,690
and calculate UV coordinates that map image
patterns.
